From ba68b7bd578b1dfd9e95f28377ea5cf109f64b91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Cipoletti <adam@amcreativecoach.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 03:49:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 68/68] Engine: the DFG reads a call's result the way this
 machine returns it

A C function hands back a 64-bit value in a pair of registers, high word
first, and the high word of a JSValue on a big-endian machine is its tag.
The baseline JIT says so at each of its own call sites; the DFG has one
place where all of its calls arrive, and it was reading the pair in the
order x86 returns it. Getting this the wrong way round does not crash:
a string comes back as a tiny denormal number, an integer as a value
whose tag is its own contents, and the wrongness surfaces somewhere else
entirely.
---
 Source/JavaScriptCore/dfg/DFGSpeculativeJIT.h | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/JavaScriptCore/dfg/DFGSpeculativeJIT.h b/Source/JavaScriptCore/dfg/DFGSpeculativeJIT.h
index d4c3067b..36b6ddc3 100644
--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/dfg/DFGSpeculativeJIT.h
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/dfg/DFGSpeculativeJIT.h
@@ -2513,6 +2513,15 @@ public:
     {
 #if USE(JSVALUE64)
         return appendCallSetResult(function, resultRegs.gpr());
+#elif CPU(PPC)
+        // A C function hands back an EncodedJSValue in a pair of registers,
+        // high word first, and the high word of a JSValue on a big-endian
+        // machine is its tag. The baseline JIT says the same thing at each of
+        // its own call sites; this is where every one of the DFG's arrives.
+        // Getting it the other way round does not crash: it returns a
+        // pointer read as a tiny denormal double, or an integer whose tag is
+        // its own value, and the wrongness surfaces somewhere else entirely.
+        return appendCallSetResult(function, resultRegs.tagGPR(), resultRegs.payloadGPR());
 #else
         return appendCallSetResult(function, resultRegs.payloadGPR(), resultRegs.tagGPR());
 #endif
